\title{Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others:\\Vision Connectors and VLA Token Compressibility}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\input{abstract}

\keywords{vision-language-action models, token pruning, robot learning}

\input{introduction}

\input{related_work}

\input{method}

\input{experiments}

\input{conclusion}

\input{limitations}

\bibliography{references}

\input{appendix}

\end{document}

\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models encode observations as hundreds of vision tokens, creating a computational bottleneck for real-time robotic control. Token pruning offers a training-free remedy, yet its effect varies dramatically across architectures: at retention ratio $k{=}0.95$, $\pi_{0.5}$ preserves 98\% task success while SmolVLA collapses to 0\%---why?

We identify the vision-language connector as the primary architectural factor governing pruning tolerance. The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token norms, computable in a single forward pass, predicts compressibility across the full architecture spectrum: from CV$\,{=}\,$0.038 (near-immune linear projection) to CV$\,{=}\,$0.314 (catastrophic pixel-shuffle collapse). Across five VLA architectures (450\,M--7\,B parameters) and four LIBERO manipulation suites, token-independent connectors degrade gracefully, while token-entangling connectors exhibit abrupt cliffs: SmolVLA collapses when just 6 of 128 tokens are removed.

Importance-based selection is not universally beneficial---it can be actively harmful. L2 norm selection preserves 98\% success on $\pi_{0.5}$ where random yields 5\% (93\,pp gap); at aggressive ratios, random outperforms L2 by up to 36\,pp. No scoring method is universally safe: task complexity amplifies connector-dependent patterns ($20{\times}$ from Object to Goal). A zero-mask ablation isolates the mechanism: zeroing 10\% of tokens preserves 98.0\% SR while physical removal collapses to 9.0\%---the 89\,pp gap is entirely attributable to position disruption, not information loss. For architectures where all deletion fails, a lightweight re-encoding module (${\sim}$5\% extra parameters) recovers $>$60\% success at 75\% compression.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/paper/fig_hero_overview.pdf}
\caption{Overview of VLA token compressibility.
\textbf{(a)}~The same robot observation produces qualitatively different token importance patterns through different vision connectors, quantified by the coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token norms.
\textbf{(b)}~Connector architecture determines pruning tolerance: independent connectors degrade gracefully, entangling connectors exhibit cliffs; task complexity amplifies the divergence.
\textbf{(c)}~A single forward pass CV diagnostic routes each architecture to an appropriate compression strategy.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure*}

Remove 5\% of vision tokens from SmolVLA and its success rate collapses from 59.2\% to near-zero (${\leq}$6.6\%). Apply the same reduction to $\pi_{0.5}$ and it retains 98.2\% under L2 norm selection. Both are Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models built on a ViT encoder feeding tokens through a connector into a language model backbone; the critical difference is the connector architecture. Token pruning is well-established for ViTs~\cite{rao2021dynamicvit,bolya2023tome,liang2022evit} and extends to VLAs~\cite{cheng2026vlaiap,xu2025vlacache,yang2025efficientvla,liu2025vlapruner}, but each method validates on a single architecture, implicitly assuming pruning transfers across designs.

Yet current VLAs employ fundamentally different vision connectors---linear projections~\cite{black2024pi0}, MLP projectors~\cite{kim2024openvla}, pixel-shuffle folding~\cite{shukor2025smolvla}, and learned encoders with cross-token self-attention~\cite{zheng2025xvla}---each imposing distinct constraints on how visual information distributes across tokens. Deploying a pruning method validated on one connector type to another risks silent degradation or complete failure.

We present an empirical study of VLA token compressibility, identifying the vision-language connector as the primary determinant of pruning robustness (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}). Token-independent connectors degrade gracefully; entangling connectors produce catastrophic failures amplified by task complexity ($20{\times}$ from Object to Goal). The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms captures this on a continuous spectrum (0.038 to 0.314). Under sufficient compression, scoring effectiveness becomes connector-dependent: L2 norm aids $\pi_{0.5}$ (up to $+$46.9\,pp on Long at $k{=}0.9$; $+$35.2\,pp on Goal at $k{=}0.85$) but reverses at aggressive ratios ($-$36\,pp at $k{=}0.5$); on X-VLA it is anti-informative ($-$8 to $-$27\,pp), while attention recovers near-baseline performance. For pixel-shuffle connectors where all pruning fails, Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR, ${\sim}$5\% extra parameters) recovers over 60\% SR at 75\% compression.

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item A compressibility analysis across five VLA architectures (450M--7B) on four LIBERO suites, identifying connector architecture as the primary determinant of pruning robustness.
    \item CV of token L2 norms as a single-pass diagnostic for connector compressibility type (uniform, graded, catastrophic), validated on four closed-loop architectures.
    \item A zero-mask ablation proving position disruption---not information loss---drives $\pi_{0.5}$'s 89\,pp failure gap. VTR recovers a pixel-shuffle VLA from 0\% to over 60\% at 75\% compression.
\end{itemize}

