\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Models and Connectors}
\label{sec:models}

We evaluate token pruning across six VLA architectures spanning three vision connector families (Table~\ref{tab:model_properties}; Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). These connectors differ in whether they process ViT patches independently or create cross-token dependencies.

\textbf{Linear projection} applies a single matrix multiplication per patch. $\pi_{0}$~\cite{black2024pi0} projects 256 patches through a linear layer; $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{black2025pi05} uses a $\times\!\sqrt{d}$-scaled linear projection across 768 tokens from three cameras (CV$\,{<}\,$0.06).

\textbf{MLP projector} maps each patch independently through a multi-layer perceptron. OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} uses a three-layer MLP (256 tokens; CV$\,{=}\,$0.215); OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft} shares the same connector (512 tokens; CV$\,{=}\,$0.219).

\textbf{Token-entangling connectors} introduce cross-token dependencies. X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} feeds tokens through a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart} (50 tokens). SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} applies pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ folding, concatenating 16 contiguous patches per output token (128 tokens).

We classify coupling as \emph{independent} when each output token depends on a single input patch, and \emph{entangling} when output tokens encode information from multiple patches. Four models are evaluated in closed-loop control on LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero}; $\pi_{0}$ and OFT contribute to the CV diagnostic only.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/paper/fig_architecture_v4.pdf}
\caption{Vision connector architectures across four closed-loop VLAs, ordered by increasing compressibility fragility. Token-independent connectors ($\pi_{0.5}$: linear; OpenVLA: MLP) yield low CV. Token-entangling connectors (X-VLA: BART encoder; SmolVLA: pixel-shuffle) create cross-token dependencies and higher CV, predicting threshold-graded or catastrophic pruning failure.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure*}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{VLA models evaluated. Coupling indicates independent vs.\ cross-token processing. $N$ = vision tokens input to the LLM. $^\dagger$OFT is an OpenVLA fine-tuning variant, included for CV calibration (offline metrics only).}
\label{tab:model_properties}
\begin{tabular}{lrlcrll}
\toprule
Model & Params & Connector & $N$ & CV & Coupling & Eval \\
\midrule
$\pi_{0.5}$ & 4B & Linear ($\times\!\sqrt{d}$) & 768 & 0.055 & Indep.\ & Closed-loop \\
X-VLA & 0.9B & Linear + BART & 50 & 0.151 & Entangling & Closed-loop \\
OpenVLA & 7B & 3-layer MLP & 256 & 0.215 & Indep.\ & Closed-loop \\
SmolVLA & 450M & Pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ & 128 & 0.314 & Entangling & Closed-loop \\
\midrule
$\pi_{0}$ & 3.3B & Linear & 256 & 0.038 & Indep.\ & Offline \\
OFT$^\dagger$ & 7B & 3-layer MLP & 512 & 0.219 & Indep.\ & Offline \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Pruning Protocol}
\label{sec:pruning_protocol}

We apply post-connector token pruning at retention ratio $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95\}$, retaining $\lfloor k \cdot N \rfloor$ tokens. Two strategies bracket expected performance: \textbf{L2 norm selection} retains the top-$k$ fraction by L2 norm, and \textbf{random subsampling} selects $\lfloor k \cdot N \rfloor$ tokens uniformly at random. The \emph{importance margin} at ratio $k$ is $\text{SR}_{\text{L2}}(k) - \text{SR}_{\text{Random}}(k)$; a positive margin indicates token importance structure is informative. Both strategies physically remove unselected tokens and re-index position IDs. We prune post-connector to isolate the connector's effect on token information structure.

\subsection{Compressibility Diagnostic: Importance CV}
\label{sec:cv_diagnostic}

We quantify post-connector token heterogeneity via the coefficient of variation (CV) of L2 norms:
\begin{equation}
\text{CV} = \frac{\sigma\!\left(\{\|t_i\|_2\}_{i=1}^{N}\right)}{\mu\!\left(\{\|t_i\|_2\}_{i=1}^{N}\right)}
\label{eq:cv}
\end{equation}
CV is a single-pass diagnostic requiring one forward pass with no downstream evaluation. It captures a continuous spectrum from $\pi_{0}$ (CV$\,{=}\,$0.038, near-uniform norms) to SmolVLA (CV$\,{=}\,$0.314, high heterogeneity). Low-CV models have near-uniform tokens where \emph{which} tokens are removed matters more than \emph{how many}; high-CV entangling connectors fail catastrophically under any removal strategy.

\subsection{Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR)}
\label{sec:vtr}

Hard pruning fails for token-entangling connectors because removing any token destroys spatially distributed information. VTR addresses this through learned re-encoding: a cross-attention module (${\sim}$22M parameters, ${\sim}$5\% of SmolVLA) uses $M$ learned queries to attend over all $N$ source tokens. The compression ratio is controlled by $M \in \{16, 32, 64, 96, 128\}$; $M{=}N$ serves as a passthrough control.

